#{[17592186351186 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3+3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3+3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+5{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}\\right),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{5},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{5}.$"] [17592186351187 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}7-5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3-5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 2{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is the same as the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}={w}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}\\right),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=1,b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $a+b=\\frac{3}{2}.$"] [17592186351188 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2+2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2+2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}\\right),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}. \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2+5t){e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186351226 "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${u}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle |{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"] [17592186351257 "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{10}b \\\\  & =b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186351261 "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-2)\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=-\\frac{4}{3},$ $c=\\frac{13}{3},$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{13}{3}\\right)}=-\\frac{4}{13}.\n$$"] [17592186351262 "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $E,$ then $E$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $E=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $E,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nE=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}\\underbrace{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 12 \\\\ 16 & 24\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186351263 "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=6,$ $c=5,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$"] [17592186351265 "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{5\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot 0+10\\cdot 3}{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{4}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{41}}{41} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{41}}{41} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{41}}{41},$ $b=-\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41},$ $c=\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|-\\frac{3\\sqrt{41}}{41}|+|-\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}|+|\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}|=\\frac{11\\sqrt{41}}{41}.\n$$"] [17592186351306 "Looking at the matrix $T$, we can see that $T$ represents a stretch in the $y$-direction of scale factor $\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $T$ is\n\n![](images/7d/7d321ec7760226fbdf2c1d539f5d9da92fa27abf84a2890702273da41df75848.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the rectangle shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186351336 "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot 2+(-9)\\cdot 1}{(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-2)\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 2+(-9)\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(-\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186351337 "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}v=-1\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186351427 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\right)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\right)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 3 & -7\\end{array}\\right] &  & 