{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{8{e}^{4t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,\\,-2{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+{(-2{e}^{2t})}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{4{e}^{4t}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,-2{e}^{2t},0⟩}{\\left(\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{4{e}^{4t}+1}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨1,\\,-2{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨2{e}^{2t},1,0⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨1,\\,-2{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2{e}^{2t} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}2{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}2{e}^{2t} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}⟨0+0,-(0-0),-4{e}^{4t}-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}⟨0,0,-(4{e}^{4t}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186363654 "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{({J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(1))} & ={e}^{\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{2}(2) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{1}(1)\\end{bmatrix}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{({J}_{2}(2))} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{({J}_{1}(1))}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{({J}_{2}(2))}\\,⊕\\,{e}^{({J}_{1}(1))}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{2}(2),$ we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda =2.$ since $n=2,$ we need to compute the first derivative of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}(2)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(2) & {f}^{′}(2) \\\\ 0 & f(2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2} & {e}^{2} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(1),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =1.$ \\since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(1)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{(1)}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}e\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{({J}_{2}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(2))} & ={e}^{({J}_{2}(1))}\\,⊕\\,{e}^{({J}_{2}(2))} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2} & {e}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & e\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186363665 "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(5))}^{2} & ={\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{1}(3) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{3}(5)\\end{bmatrix}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{({J}_{1}(3))}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {({J}_{3}(5))}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={({J}_{1}(3))}^{2}\\,⊕\\,{({J}_{3}(5))}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(3),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =3.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(3)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{3}(5),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivatives of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(5)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(5) & {f}^{′}(5) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(5)}{2} \\\\ 0 & f(5) & {f}^{′}(5) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(5)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{5}^{2} & 2\\cdot 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & {5}^{2} & 2\\cdot 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {5}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}25 & 10 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 25 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 25\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(5)) & =({J}_{1}(3))\\,⊕\\,({J}_{3}(5)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 25 & 10 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 25 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 25\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365003 "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\right)}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365066 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{x} & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T