{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\hat{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-(-1))^{2}+(2-(-1))^{2}+(5-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{27} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369720 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\right)}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{9}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 3+0\\cdot (-1)}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{13}\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{13}}\\right)≈{23.09}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."] [17592186369727 "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -6 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right)}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
